    <id>2</id>
    <title>Combined summary - [BIP Draft] Segregated Data: a prunable, script-isolated block region for data carriage</title>
    <updated>2026-07-29T04:21:10.109080+00:00</updated>
    <link href="https://delvingbitcoin.org/t/bip-draft-segregated-data-a-prunable-script-isolated-block-region-for-data-carriage/2641/46" rel="alternate"/>
    <published>2026-06-23 16:40:23.660000+00:00</published>
    <summary>The Segregated Data (SegData) proposal introduces a new method for handling data within Bitcoin blocks, aiming to optimize storage without transferring value. This system segregates certain types of data, traditionally placed in OP_RETURN or crammed into witness sections, allowing them to be pruned after validation without affecting the blockchain's integrity. The design, inspired by BIP-141, includes modifications like a distinct Merkle root for data entries and an updated block serialization process. SegData is particularly suitable for applications like indexing non-transactional data but does not support script-evaluated data like covenants due to its structure.
Potential adopters of SegData include services that require timestamping and data attestation, which do not need to engage directly with transactional elements of the blockchain. The implementation of SegData could lead to more efficient use of blockchain resources, reducing the burden on full nodes which do not have to retain non-essential data indefinitely. This adjustment could potentially lower the cost and resource consumption associated with node operations, making the network more accessible for smaller participants or those with limited capacity.
However, several open questions remain concerning the implementation specifics of SegData, such as the management of version and length slots for witness v2 and the granularity of coverage tiers for peer services. These aspects highlight the need for careful consideration in balancing node resource management with privacy and efficiency. Additionally, the feasibility of integrating such a system relies heavily on community acceptance and the practical benefits it offers over existing methods like OP_RETURN transactions, which are currently utilized for similar purposes without requiring consensus changes.
In summary, while SegData presents a technically sound proposal that aligns with the principles of efficiency and decentralization inherent in blockchain technology, its adoption and practical implementation may hinge on broader community support and demonstrated advantages over current practices. The ongoing discussions and updates to the proposal will likely play a crucial role in shaping its future use within the Bitcoin ecosystem.</summary>
